/*!
    \page creator-how-to-view-android-logcat.html
    \previouspage creator-how-tos.html

    \ingroup creator-how-to-android

    \title View Android logcat output

    Android reports what happens on a device in a system log called logcat. Use
    the \uicontrol Logcat tab in \l {Application Output} to read it while your
    application runs, and to filter it down to the messages that help you debug.

    \section1 Get started

    To follow the log of a device or an emulator:

    \list 1
        \li Connect the device and \l{Debug on Android devices}
            {enable USB debugging on it}, or \l {Start AVDs}{start an
            emulator}.
        \li Go to \uicontrol Tools > \uicontrol Android >
            \uicontrol Logcat and select the device. The menu lists the
            devices that are ready to use.
    \endlist

    \image {qtcreator-android-logcat.webp} {Logcat tab in Application Output}

    Each device has its own tab. To check which device a tab shows, hover over
    the tab. The tooltip displays the name, SDK level, and serial number of the
    device.

    When you run an application on a device, \QC opens the tab for you and
    filters it to the messages of that application. Stopping or re-running the
    application in the tab stops or re-runs it on the device.

    \QC reads the log while the tab is visible and while an application that
    you started from \QC is running on the device. If you hide the tab or the
    \uicontrol {Application Output} pane while nothing is running, \QC stops
    reading the log, and the messages that the device logs in the meantime are
    lost. When you show the tab again, a status message tells you that output
    was skipped, and \QC continues from there.

    \section1 Read the log

    In the \uicontrol Standard view, each line looks like this:

    \code
    2026-10-06 12:32:38.084 HWUI                    io.qt.coffeemachine                  W  Image decoding logging dropped!
    \endcode

    From left to right, the columns show when the message was logged, its tag,
    the package name of the application that logged it, its level, and the
    message text. You can also show the IDs of the process and the thread.

    \QC shows the level as a letter and colors the whole line to match:

    \table
    \header
        \li Letter
        \li Level
        \li Meaning
    \row
        \li \c V
        \li Verbose
        \li Fine-grained details, useful only while you trace a problem.
    \row
        \li \c D
        \li Debug
        \li Diagnostic messages that developers add for debugging.
    \row
        \li \c I
        \li Info
        \li Normal events, such as a service starting.
    \row
        \li \c W
        \li Warning
        \li An unexpected situation that the application handled, but that
            might cause problems later.
    \row
        \li \c E
        \li Error
        \li An operation failed.
    \row
        \li \c F
        \li Fatal
        \li An unrecoverable failure. The application usually ends right
            after it.
    \endtable

    A message that spans several lines, such as a stack trace, shows the columns
    only on its first line. The other lines are indented, so that the message
    text stays aligned.

    \section2 Status messages

    Between the log messages, \QC adds lines of its own, enclosed in asterisks,
    such as \c {**** Phone_API_36 (emulator-5554) - disconnected ****}. They
    report that:

    \list
        \li The device disconnected or connected again. After a disconnect,
            \QC resumes reading the log by itself when the device returns.
        \li \QC skipped output while the tab was hidden.
        \li \QC could not start \c adb.
    \endlist

    These lines are shown whatever the filter is.

    \section1 Choose what each line shows

    There are two layouts, and they apply to all \uicontrol Logcat tabs. To
    choose one, go to \preferences > \uicontrol {Build & Run} >
    \uicontrol {Application Output} and set \uicontrol {View mode} in the
    \uicontrol {Android Logcat} group.

    \image {qtcreator-preferences-android-logcat.webp} {Android Logcat group in Application Output preferences}

    \list
        \li \uicontrol Compact is short and to the point. It shows the level,
            the tag, the ID of the process, and the message:

            \code
            I/ActivityManager( 1483): Start proc 4521:com.example.app
            \endcode
        \li \uicontrol Standard lets you decide which columns to show. Select
            them with \uicontrol {Show date and time},
            \uicontrol {Show process and thread IDs}, \uicontrol {Show tag},
            and \uicontrol {Show package name}. All except the process and
            thread IDs are on by default. The level and the message always
            follow.
    \endlist

    The column options are available only in the \uicontrol Standard layout.
    The columns have a fixed width, so that the messages line up. If a tag or a
    package name does not fit, \QC replaces the middle of it with three dots.

    Logcat tabs also follow these settings in the \uicontrol {Application
    Output} preferences:

    \list
        \li \uicontrol {Word-wrap output} wraps long messages, so that you do
            not have to scroll sideways.
        \li \uicontrol {Limit output to} sets how much output \QC keeps for a
            tab. When the limit is reached, \QC discards the oldest messages.
        \li \uicontrol {Overwrite background color} sets a background color
            for the output.
    \endlist

    \section1 Filter messages

    A Logcat tab shows the messages of the whole system, not only those of your
    application. To narrow them down, enter a query in the filter field of the
    \uicontrol {Application Output} pane. Each tab has its own filter, and \QC
    applies it to the messages that the tab already contains, too.

    A query consists of terms separated by spaces. A message is shown when it
    matches all the terms. The matching is not case sensitive.

    \section2 Query keys

    A term is either plain text or a key with a value, such as
    \c {tag:NetworkManager}.

    \table
    \header
        \li Term
        \li Shows messages that
    \row
        \li \e text
        \li contain \e text anywhere in the line, including the tag and the
            package name.
    \row
        \li \c {tag:}\e name
        \li have a tag that contains \e name.
    \row
        \li \c {package:}\e name
        \li come from an application that has \e name in its package name.
    \row
        \li \c package:mine
        \li come from the application of the active project, including its
            other processes.
    \row
        \li \c {level:}\e level
        \li have the given level or a more severe one. Use \c verbose,
            \c debug, \c info, \c warning, \c error, or \c fatal. You can
            shorten them, as in \c {level:warn}. \c assert means the same as
            \c fatal.
    \endtable

    \section2 Exclude messages

    To leave messages out, put a minus sign in front of a term. This works for
    keys and for plain text. For example, to hide the messages of the Wi-Fi
    daemon, enter:

    \code
    -tag:wpa_supplicant
    \endcode

    \section2 Combine terms

    Each term narrows the result further. For example, to see the warnings and
    errors of your application, except those with the tag \c Choreographer,
    enter:

    \code
    package:mine level:warning -tag:Choreographer
    \endcode

    The following queries are useful starting points:

    \table
    \header
        \li To see
        \li Enter
    \row
        \li All messages of your application
        \li \c package:mine
    \row
        \li Only the problems of your application
        \li \c {package:mine level:error}
    \row
        \li The messages of one component
        \li \c {tag:NetworkManager}
    \row
        \li Messages that mention an exception
        \li \c exception
    \row
        \li The messages of a package, without the noisy ones
        \li \c {package:com.example -tag:Choreographer}
    \endtable

    \section2 About package names

    The log does not contain the package name of a message. \QC looks it up in
    the list of the processes that run on the device. A package name can
    therefore appear a few seconds after its process has started, and until then
    \c {package:} terms do not match the messages of that process.

    \c package:mine matches nothing until \QC knows the package name of the
    active project, which is after you have built the project or run it on the
    device.

    \section1 Find crashes

    You do not have to search the log for crashes. When you run an application
    on an Android device, \QC watches the log and lists the following problems
    in \uicontrol Issues:

    \list
        \li A native crash.
        \li A Java \c {FATAL EXCEPTION}, with the stack trace in the details
            of the issue.
        \li The termination of the application by the Android low-memory
            killer, with the name and the process ID of the application.
    \endlist

    \section1 Troubleshooting

    \list
        \li \b {The device is not in the Logcat menu.} The menu lists only the
            devices that are ready to use. Check that USB debugging is enabled
            and that you have authorized the computer on the device. Also check
            whether the device is listed in \preferences >
            \uicontrol Devices.
        \li \b {The tab is empty.} Check the filter field. A query that no
            message matches, such as \c package:mine before you have built the
            project, hides everything.
        \li \b {Messages are missing.} If the tab contains the status message
            that output was skipped, the tab was hidden while nothing was
            running, and \QC did not read the log in the meantime. Keep the tab
            visible to see all messages.
    \endlist

    \sa {Android}{How to: Develop for Android}, {Developing for Android},
    {Application Output}, {Debug on Android devices}
*/
